1
00:00:00,566 --> 00:00:03,733
Just out of the subway you spend six yuan on a can of ice cold cola

2
00:00:04,033 --> 00:00:08,200
The tab pops and that first sip feels like a cool breeze finally reaching your throat

3
00:00:08,533 --> 00:00:19,400
Five minutes later you're back at the same vending machine looking at another identical six yuan can and thinking the last one was worth it so this one must be too

4
00:00:19,633 --> 00:00:22,966
But what you're about to pay for may not be the second can

5
00:00:23,233 --> 00:00:25,333
It may be that first sip that's already gone

6
00:00:25,566 --> 00:00:27,266
The first sip is already over

7
00:00:27,266 --> 00:00:29,366
The second can can only answer a new question

8
00:00:29,966 --> 00:00:31,866
How thirsty are you now

9
00:00:32,033 --> 00:00:35,033
This is Pixel Breath's 100 Principles of Economics

10
00:00:35,033 --> 00:00:37,900
Number 5 the law of diminishing marginal utility

11
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:41,500
No definitions to memorize just one everyday calculation

12
00:00:41,633 --> 00:00:43,733
Today we're only calculating the next can

13
00:00:44,166 --> 00:00:45,766
The first can is already gone

14
00:00:45,766 --> 00:00:47,333
The empty can is still beaded with condensation

15
00:00:47,333 --> 00:00:51,233
The second can behind the glass looks almost exactly like the first

16
00:00:51,233 --> 00:00:52,200
Same brand

17
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:53,200
Same size

18
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:54,766
Still six yuan

19
00:00:54,766 --> 00:00:58,666
So you assume that if the product hasn't changed its value to you hasn't either

20
00:00:59,033 --> 00:01:00,566
That assumption feels natural

21
00:01:00,566 --> 00:01:02,833
Because you can see the can and the price tag

22
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:07,266
What you can easily miss is that after the first can you have changed

23
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:10,766
When you bought the first can you had just stepped out of the stuffy subway

24
00:01:10,766 --> 00:01:14,733
Your mouth was dry your back was sweaty and you wanted something cold right away

25
00:01:14,833 --> 00:01:18,066
Those six yuan first met a very large need

26
00:01:18,233 --> 00:01:22,366
But by the time the second can appears you're not nearly as thirsty

27
00:01:22,433 --> 00:01:26,733
The two cans look the same but the needs they meet are different

28
00:01:26,833 --> 00:01:29,700
Economics counts these two amounts of satisfaction separately

29
00:01:29,966 --> 00:01:33,700
How much better you feel after both cans is called total utility

30
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,400
What the second can adds on its own is called marginal utility

31
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:39,366
Marginal can sound a little confusing

32
00:01:39,733 --> 00:01:43,400
In plain English it means what more will one extra give me

33
00:01:43,666 --> 00:01:47,266
Diminishing marginal utility doesn't mean the second can is suddenly useless

34
00:01:47,266 --> 00:01:50,666
Nor does total satisfaction start falling after the first can

35
00:01:51,066 --> 00:01:55,200
As long as the second can feels good your total satisfaction keeps rising

36
00:01:55,400 --> 00:02:00,133
It's just that the satisfaction added by this can is usually smaller than the one before it

37
00:02:00,466 --> 00:02:03,166
This is exactly where your calculation can go wrong

38
00:02:03,233 --> 00:02:07,133
You credit the second can in advance for the relief the first one already delivered

39
00:02:07,466 --> 00:02:08,566
The cola hasn't changed

40
00:02:08,566 --> 00:02:09,700
The price hasn't changed

41
00:02:09,766 --> 00:02:12,200
So why does the added satisfaction shrink

42
00:02:13,966 --> 00:02:18,066
Because the satisfaction a drink gives you depends on more than the drink itself

43
00:02:18,166 --> 00:02:22,200
It also depends on what you need when that drink arrives

44
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:25,100
Before the first can your most urgent need was thirst

45
00:02:25,266 --> 00:02:28,100
It filled part of that biggest gap first

46
00:02:28,366 --> 00:02:32,266
After you drink it the change isn't just one less can in the machine

47
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:35,466
Your state has changed from very thirsty to not so thirsty

48
00:02:35,733 --> 00:02:39,466
When the second can arrives it faces only the need that's left

49
00:02:39,733 --> 00:02:43,566
The next can can't fill a gap the previous one already filled

50
00:02:43,766 --> 00:02:47,600
So the product can stay the same while the added satisfaction gets smaller

51
00:02:47,733 --> 00:02:51,266
This isn't just a curve someone drew in a textbook

52
00:02:51,666 --> 00:02:57,733
A study published in 2020 examined how 290 adults felt while eating chocolate

53
00:02:58,066 --> 00:03:01,900
Researchers gave the participants the same kind of chocolate one piece at a time

54
00:03:02,166 --> 00:03:06,366
After each piece they rated the taste and could stop whenever they wanted

55
00:03:06,666 --> 00:03:11,733
Some ate two pieces while others ate fifty one with an average of about twelve

56
00:03:12,033 --> 00:03:17,866
On average their taste ratings fell with each additional piece of chocolate

57
00:03:17,866 --> 00:03:23,266
But this study measured the taste of repeatedly eating chocolate in a particular setting

58
00:03:23,433 --> 00:03:30,933
It isn't a universal scale of happiness and doesn't prove every person and product decline at the same rate

59
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,600
The conclusion we can safely take away is narrow

60
00:03:34,233 --> 00:03:46,300
In this setting when participants ate chocolate repeatedly over a short period their average taste experience gradually weakened with each later piece

61
00:03:46,666 --> 00:03:50,166
Back at the vending machine judging the second can also comes with conditions

62
00:03:50,333 --> 00:03:56,700
We're talking about the same person consuming the same thing again within a short time for the same purpose

63
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:02,366
If the first can was small and you're still thirsty then the second can may be well worth it

64
00:04:02,966 --> 00:04:09,733
If the second is for a friend or for tomorrow then the person the time and the purpose have all changed

65
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:13,933
You can't call it less worthwhile just because it comes second

66
00:04:14,266 --> 00:04:17,800
Diminishing marginal utility isn't a rule that says you may only buy one

67
00:04:18,033 --> 00:04:20,066
It only reminds you of one thing

68
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:23,300
The second can can't automatically inherit the first can's value

69
00:04:23,566 --> 00:04:28,933
So when your finger hovers over the buy button how should you judge those six yuan

70
00:04:29,466 --> 00:04:30,666
You don't need to draw a curve

71
00:04:30,666 --> 00:04:33,533
And you don't need to give happiness an exact score

72
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:36,133
Before you pay again ask yourself one thing

73
00:04:36,233 --> 00:04:39,666
What can another can still solve for you right now

74
00:04:39,933 --> 00:04:42,400
Don't let the pop of the last tab answer for it

75
00:04:42,533 --> 00:04:43,900
How cold that first sip was

76
00:04:44,133 --> 00:04:46,466
How worthwhile those six yuan felt

77
00:04:46,733 --> 00:04:49,466
Those were jobs the first can already completed

78
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:53,933
The second can must name a new price based on the need you still have

79
00:04:54,333 --> 00:04:55,533
Are you still very thirsty

80
00:04:55,866 --> 00:04:58,766
Do you still have a long walk under the sun ahead

81
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:01,900
How much new comfort can this can bring

82
00:05:02,333 --> 00:05:06,333
Is that extra comfort still worth six yuan to you right now

83
00:05:07,433 --> 00:05:10,900
If that extra comfort is still worth six yuan then buy it

84
00:05:11,366 --> 00:05:14,900
Understanding economics isn't about deliberately enjoying less

85
00:05:15,166 --> 00:05:19,800
But if all you can think is how great that last can felt then pause for ten seconds

86
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:24,533
Because you may only be trying to buy back a feeling the first can already gave you

87
00:05:24,933 --> 00:05:27,800
Diminishing marginal utility doesn't decide whether you should buy

88
00:05:27,966 --> 00:05:33,800
It simply separates the satisfaction the last can already gave you from what the next can can still add

89
00:05:33,833 --> 00:05:36,133
What I've already received belongs to the last can's account

90
00:05:36,266 --> 00:05:39,466
What the next can can still add is the account in front of me

91
00:05:39,833 --> 00:05:41,766
The vending machine's blue light is still glowing

92
00:05:41,766 --> 00:05:43,733
You drop the empty can into the recycling slot

93
00:05:43,933 --> 00:05:45,833
The second can is still behind the glass

94
00:05:45,833 --> 00:05:47,666
Maybe you'll press the button again

95
00:05:47,866 --> 00:05:50,366
Maybe you'll turn away and catch the evening breeze

96
00:05:50,433 --> 00:05:52,766
Neither choice has a standard answer

97
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,866
As long as what's paying for the second can is the need you still have now

98
00:05:56,866 --> 00:05:58,766
Not the echo left by the first sip

99
00:05:59,066 --> 00:06:02,933
Next time we'll take a new calculation to a milk tea shop

100
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,700
And see why hiring one more person can sometimes make the shop slower

